{"verdict":"approve","summary":"Second round: checking only whether the fixes add new false hits or misses. I read the store, the loader and the tests first.","findings":[],"not_checked":[],"not_expanded":0}{"verdict":"approve","summary":"Some of the checks depend on real timing. I ran the suspicious paths against the store with a fake clock.","findings":[],"not_checked":[],"not_expanded":0}{"verdict":"request_changes","summary":"The fixes still leave expired entries readable and a loader that runs twice for the same key; delete() also leaks keys in the eviction order.","findings":[{"severity":"major","file":"src/cache/store.py","line":42,"quote":"    if key in self._entries and not self._expired(key):\n        return self._entries[key]","claim":"The expiry check reads the clock once per call, but the entry's deadline is stored in seconds while the clock returns milliseconds.","failure_scenario":"An entry with a 60 second TTL is written. Two minutes later get() still returns it, because the deadline compares 60 against 120000.","suggestion":"Store deadlines in the same unit the clock returns, or convert once in _expired()."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/cache/store.py","line":57,"quote":"        self._entries.pop(key, None)","claim":"delete() removes the entry but leaves its key in the eviction order list, so the list keeps growing.","failure_scenario":"A worker that writes and deletes 10000 distinct keys keeps 10000 dead keys in _order; evict() then pops dead keys and evicts nothing.","suggestion":"Remove the key from _order in delete(), or skip keys missing from _entries in evict()."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/cache/store.py","line":73,"quote":"    def put(self, key, value, ttl=None):\n        self._entries[key] = value","claim":"put() replaces the value but keeps the old deadline when the key already exists.","failure_scenario":"A key written with ttl=1 is rewritten with ttl=3600. One second later the new value expires.","suggestion":"Reset the deadline on every put(), using the new ttl or the default."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/cache/store.py","line":88,"quote":"        with self._lock:\n            snapshot = dict(self._entries)","claim":"items() copies the entries under the lock but filters expired ones after releasing it, reading _deadlines without the lock.","failure_scenario":"One thread iterates items() while another calls put(); the deadlines dict changes size during iteration and raises RuntimeError.","suggestion":"Copy the deadlines together with the entries while holding the lock."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/cache/store.py","line":104,"quote":"    size = sum(len(v) for v in self._entries.values())","claim":"size_bytes() calls len() on every value, which counts characters for str and items for lists, not bytes.","failure_scenario":"A cache of 100 short lists reports 300 bytes and never triggers the memory limit, while it really holds several megabytes.","suggestion":"Track the encoded size at put() time and keep a running total."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/cache/store.py","line":120,"quote":"        if self._loader is not None:\n            value = self._loader(key)","claim":"get_or_load() calls the loader outside the lock, so two threads that miss the same key both load it.","failure_scenario":"Two requests for the same{"verdict":"request_changes","summary":"The cache still serves stale entries in several everyday paths: units of the deadline differ, put() keeps an old deadline, and a failing loader is called again on every request.","findings":[{"severity":"major","file":"src/cache/store.py","line":42,"quote":"    if key in self._entries and not self._expired(key):\n        return self._entries[key]","claim":"The expiry check reads the clock once per call, but the entry's deadline is stored in seconds while the clock returns milliseconds.","failure_scenario":"An entry with a 60 second TTL is written. Two minutes later get() still returns it, because the deadline compares 60 against 120000.","suggestion":"Store deadlines in the same unit the clock returns, or convert once in _expired()."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/cache/store.py","line":57,"quote":"        self._entries.pop(key, None)","claim":"delete() removes the entry but leaves its key in the eviction order list, so the list keeps growing.","failure_scenario":"A worker that writes and deletes 10000 distinct keys keeps 10000 dead keys in _order; evict() then pops dead keys and evicts nothing.","suggestion":"Remove the key from _order in delete(), or skip keys missing from _entries in evict()."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/cache/store.py","line":73,"quote":"    def put(self, key, value, ttl=None):\n        self._entries[key] = value","claim":"put() replaces the value but keeps the old deadline when the key already exists.","failure_scenario":"A key written with ttl=1 is rewritten with ttl=3600. One second later the new value expires.","suggestion":"Reset the deadline on every put(), using the new ttl or the default."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/cache/store.py","line":88,"quote":"        with self._lock:\n            snapshot = dict(self._entries)","claim":"items() copies the entries under the lock but filters expired ones after releasing it, reading _deadlines without the lock.","failure_scenario":"One thread iterates items() while another calls put(); the deadlines dict changes size during iteration and raises RuntimeError.","suggestion":"Copy the deadlines together with the entries while holding the lock."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/cache/store.py","line":104,"quote":"    size = sum(len(v) for v in self._entries.values())","claim":"size_bytes() calls len() on every value, which counts characters for str and items for lists, not bytes.","failure_scenario":"A cache of 100 short lists reports 300 bytes and never triggers the memory limit, while it really holds several megabytes.","suggestion":"Track the encoded size at put() time and keep a running total."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/cache/store.py","line":120,"quote":"        if self._loader is not None:\n            value = self._loader(key)","claim":"get_or_load() calls the loader outside the lock, so two threads that miss the same key both load it.","failure_scenario":"Two requests for the same missing key arrive together; the loader runs twice and the second result overwrites the first.","suggestion":"Keep a per-key in-flight marker so the second caller waits for the first load."},{"severity":"major","file":"src/cache/store.py","line":131,"quote":"        except Exception:\n            return None","claim":"get_or_load() turns every loader error into a miss and caches nothing, so a failing backend is called again on every request.","failure_scenario":"The backend is down. Each of 500 requests per second calls the loader, which times out after 5 seconds; all threads block on it.","suggestion":"Cache the failure for a short back-off period and re-raise to the caller."}],"not_checked":[],"not_expanded":2}
